ly on brute  search made it less vulnerable to  machine dominance in a game like chess  it's thought to be the most complex game  ever invented and mastered by our  species the pinnacle of our intelligence  and the one skill that computers  couldn't match until 2016 that's when a  network of hundreds of computers at  Google controlled by the artificial  intelligence named alphago beat the best  go player of the decade we siedel go a  game that arguably requires the best  that human minds have to offer strategy  judgment adaptability beat so is it time  to hang up our hats and give in to the  domination by our computer overlords  lurking just around the corner  wait a minute you might be thinking  wasn't this lecture about how technology  is making us smarter not the other way  around  indeed it is one of the go players who  helped train alphago by playing against  it  Fong Hui has said that the AI made him a  better player showing him possibilities  that he had never seen before we already  know that music algorithms can write or  atleast evaluate pop songs and these  algorithms can even make human composers  more creative giving them access to many  more soundscapes and experiments of  music than would be possible without  them so to sum up technology opens new  frontiers to humans and it's natural for  people to have concerns and even  anxieties about what may happen as we  explore these new frontiers but when it  comes to the question do today's  technologies make us stupid well based  on the evidence we'd have to say no  rather our brains are adapting to the  new skills that technology will demand  of us remembering where to find  information for example in favor of the  facts themselves and with that thought  we've come to the end of our lecture  series we've explored a number of  fascinating topics in  course but to pull it all together let  me leave you with three fundamental  principles that I hope will stay with  you and help you build a scaffold upon  whic