other than to be fairly convinced that  he will be sub leash subtly shaped by  all this documentation at the end of the  day technology is a tool that can  dramatically expand our capacity to  store and retrieve information if you  think of the internet as an extension of  our memory for example then there's no  question that it is a vast improvement  over the capacity of any individual  human brain but some people still balk  at the idea that technology can enhance  certain characteristically human  functions like creativity if you're in  that camp of skeptics I'd like you to  consider the story of alphago back in  1997 the world was shocked when deep  blue an artificial intelligence system  built by IBM beat the reigning world  chess champion Garry Kasparov this event  served as a kind of turning point when  we had to admit that AI was catching up  to human intelligence at least in  specific ways no one today would argue  that you can't build an artificial  intelligence machine that can best any  human in chess so people who believe  that a I can never simulate the human  mind began to point to the ancient  Chinese game of go to demonstrate human  superiority over artificial intelligence  some pointed to go as the last frontier  the last game in which humans could beat  machines the oldest board game still  played today go has been around for  thousands of years it's a two-player  game in which each player places stones  on intersecting lines in a grid without  getting into the rules suffice it to say  that go is considered far more  complicated than chess because of the  number of alternatives for each play  some have said that there are more  possibilities and go than there are  atoms in the visible universe there are  a lot of atoms in the visible universe  so I'm a bit doubtful but the scope of  options and go is unquestionably fast  because searching through algorithms and  stores of data is how  I can easily beat out humans the fact  that go skill doesn't rely on brute  search made it less vulnerable to  machine dominance in a game like chess  it's thought to be the most complex game  ever invented and mastered by our  species the pinnacle of our intelligence  and the one skill that computers  couldn't match until 2016 that's when a  network of hundreds of computers at  Google controlled by the artificial  intelligence named alphago beat the best  go player of the decade we siedel go a  game that arguably requires the best  that human minds have to offer strategy  judgment adaptability beat so is it time  to hang up our hats and give in to the  domination by our computer overlords  lurking just around the corner  wait a minute you might be thinking  wasn't this lecture about how technology  is making us smarter not the other way  around  indeed it is one of the go players who  helped train alphago by playing against  it  Fong Hui has said that the AI made him a  better player showing him possibilities  that he had never seen before we already  know that music algorithms can write or  atleast evaluate pop songs and these  algorithms can even make human composers  more creative giving them access to many  more soundscapes and experiments of  music than would be possible without  them so to sum up technology opens new  frontiers to humans and it's natural for  people to have concerns and even  anxieties about what may happen as we  explore these new frontiers but when it  comes to the question do today's  technologies make us stupid well based  on the evidence we'd have to say no  rather our brains are adapting to the  new skills that technology will demand  of us remembering where to find  information for example in favor of the  facts themselves and with that thought  we've come to the end of our lecture  series we've explored a number of  fascinating topics in  course but to pull it all together let  me leave you with three fundamental  principles that I hope will stay with  you and help you build a scaffold upon  which you can hang new information about  the brain as its discovered and reported  first remember that the brain is not  perfectly designed it's messy random  inefficient full of shortcuts that allow  us to think quickly but also lead us to  make mistakes from the lowest levels of  perception to the highest levels of  decision-making second I hope you've  learned that no brain region is an  island the power of the brain is in its  inner connectedness and the mailee  ability with which it wires and rewires  with experience and finally we've seen  that our thought processes can be  divided into two systems we have a fast  thinking automatic set of processes that  make up what we often call a gut  instinct of which we are largely unaware  and we have a deliberate slow rational  set of processes that we think dominate  our actions more than they likely do  many many of our judgments arise from  our fast thinking system and sometimes  those judgments can have negative  consequences both for individuals and  for society at large but whether we're  talking about racial bias optical  delusions vivid remembering or any other  brain function we can choose to bring  that slow thinking system into play  we're lucky enough to be able to check  our actions choose to act responsibly  and ethically and apologize for our past  mistakes science continues to unravel  the many mysteries of the brain and with  each new discovery the story just gets  more interesting we focused on  dismissing some of the myths that  surround our mental lives but in the  process I hope I've only enriched your  sense of wonder at that amazing and  often baffling thing the human brain and  I hope that you will leave this course  with a better sense of how your own mind  work and how it will continue to change  in the future


